      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Thresholding playground\n\nThe same denoised grayscale goes through every classic tactic side by side. The photo is a book page with a light shaft across it, so the page is bright on one side and nearly black on the other - there is no single grey level that separates ink from paper anywhere in the frame:\n\n- MANUAL GLOBAL: cv2.threshold with a fixed value (127) - simple, but the right value depends on the photo. Here it swallows the whole shaded half.\n- OTSU / TRIANGLE: the threshold is computed from the histogram (Otsu: best two-class split, great for bimodal histograms; Triangle: distance to the histogram peak, good for skewed unimodal ones). The float output is the value they picked - inspect it. Both are still ONE number for the whole image, so both fail on this page.\n- ADAPTIVE MEAN / GAUSSIAN: every pixel is compared to its local neighborhood (31x31 minus C) - the first tactic that survives uneven lighting. C is a fixed offset, so in the deepest shadow, where the ink/paper gap shrinks below C, the text disappears into blank white.\n- NIBLACK / SAUVOLA (cv2.ximgproc.niBlackThreshold): the local threshold is mean + k * stddev over the same 31x31 window, i.e. the offset SCALES with local contrast instead of being fixed - which is why the shaded text comes back. Niblack uses a negative k (~-0.2) for dark text and speckles flat paper, because in a textureless area stddev is tiny and it binarizes noise. Sauvola normalizes by r (the expected stddev range, 128 for 8-bit) with a positive k (~0.2), which leaves flat paper alone. The dropdown also offers WOLF and NICK - flip the sign of k if an output comes out solid.\n- THRESHOLD SWEEP: 'range' (Basic data handling: DataListRange) emits the integers 0..252 (step 28) as a ComfyUI LIST - everything downstream runs once per value. A 'to FLOAT' cast feeds cv2.threshold's 'thresh' input (converted from a widget; range is generic control flow, so no CV-specific sweep node is needed), so one node call per threshold comes out as a list of images: the preview iterates them and the inspect prints each value. The 'Threshold sweep sheet' SUBGRAPH labels each tile with its own threshold (CastToString + Draw Text) and returns the list, so you can read the best global value off the strip instead of guessing - and see that on this photo none of them is good. Change the threshold type inside the subgraph to sweep THRESH_TRUNC / THRESH_TOZERO instead.\n\nEverything stays NPARRAY after one Image -> CV Array (GRAY) conversion."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
